<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vincent van Gogh</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1853">1853</a>–<a href="/years/1890">1890</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Van Gogh</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vincent van Gogh was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> Post-Impressionist painter whose expressive colour and brushwork made him one of the most influential artists of modern times. He painted most of his works, among them The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potato">Potato</a> Eaters, Sunflowers and The Starry Night, in about a decade, much of it in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Little recognised in his lifetime and troubled by mental illness, he died in 1890 at Auvers-sur-Oise.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vincent-van-gogh.jpg" alt="Vincent van Gogh" width="320" height="404" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vincent van Gogh · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vincent_van_Gogh_-_Self-Portrait_-_Google_Art_Project_(454045).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Vincent Willem van Gogh was born on 30 March 1853 in Groot-Zundert, in the predominantly Catholic province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-brabant">North Brabant</a>. He was the oldest surviving child of Theodorus van Gogh, a minister of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-reformed-church">Dutch Reformed Church</a>, and Anna Cornelia Carbentus. His grandfather, also named Vincent, was a prominent art dealer, and three of that grandfather's six sons became art dealers. His younger brother Theo was born on 1 May 1857. Van Gogh was taught at home, then sent to boarding school at Zevenbergen and in 1866 to the middle school in Tilburg, where he was deeply unhappy. He returned home in March 1868 and later wrote that his youth was &quot;austere and cold, and sterile.&quot;</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>As an adult he began painting at an elementary level. In early 1882 his uncle Cornelis Marinus, owner of a gallery in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a>, commissioned drawings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-hague">The Hague</a> but was twice disappointed. Theo gave him money for materials in August 1882. From early 1883 Van Gogh worked on multi-figure compositions, and he destroyed several large paintings made at Nuenen; The Potato Eaters and its companion pieces are the only ones to survive. In November 1885 he travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a> and later to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, where he met Émile Bernard and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-gauguin">Paul Gauguin</a>. His colours grew brighter, and his style was fully realised during his stay in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arles">Arles</a> in 1888, when he painted series of olive trees, wheat fields and sunflowers.</p><p>The correspondence with Theo is the most comprehensive primary source on his work. It includes more than 600 letters from Vincent to Theo, and most of what is known of his theories of art is recorded in them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Van Gogh suffered from psychotic episodes and delusions. His friendship with Gauguin ended after a confrontation with a razor, when he severed part of his own left ear. He spent time in psychiatric hospitals, including one at Saint-Rémy, and then moved to the Auberge Ravoux in Auvers-sur-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oise">Oise</a>, where he came under the care of Dr Paul Gachet. On 27 July 1890 he shot himself in the chest and died two days later.</p><p>His reputation grew steadily among artists and critics after exhibitions in the late 1880s. Theo died in January 1891, and Theo's widow Jo van Gogh-Bonger took charge of the paintings and letters. Memorial exhibitions were held in several cities, and in 1896 the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henri-matisse">Henri Matisse</a> was introduced to Van Gogh's work and turned to bright colours. The Fauves and German Expressionists later took up elements of his style.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vincent van Gogh” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Dutch water engineering, social policies such as the first legal same-sex marriages in 2001, and artists from <b>Vincent van Gogh</b> to Piet Mondrian have had worldwide influence.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Kingdom of the Netherlands · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>During the 1880s younger painters, <b>Vincent van Gogh</b>, Paul Gauguin, Georges Seurat and Henri de Toulouse-Lautrec, reacted against the Impressionists’ concern with reproducing the optical sensations of light and colour and turned to symbolic content and the expression of emotion.</blockquote><a href="/movement/impressionism">Impressionism · Outcomes</a></li><li><blockquote>After a quarrel with Paul Gauguin in Arles, <b>Vincent van Gogh</b> cut off part of his left ear.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-23">23 December in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Zundert, in what is now <a href="/countries/netherlands">the Netherlands</a>. Died in Auvers-sur-Oise, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.47,4.66&amp;year=1877" aria-label="Zundert on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4655 382 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Vincent van Gogh"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5105 652h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5105 652h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.47,4.66&amp;year=1877">What was here in 1877? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;And withal he ought to have some feeling for the stars and the infinite heavens. Then it is a joy to live!&#8221;</p><footer>Letter from Arles, 1888 (The Letters of a Post-Impressionist) &#183; tr. Anthony M. Ludovici, 1912 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/40393/pg40393-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;But when shall I paint the starry heavens?—that picture which is always in my mind?&#8221;</p><footer>Letter from Arles, 1888 (The Letters of a Post-Impressionist) &#183; tr. Anthony M. Ludovici, 1912 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/40393/pg40393-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;A violet city, yellow stars, a green-blue sky, crops of all colours, old-gold, copper, green-gold, red-gold, yellow-gold, yellow-bronze, green and red. I painted it in the midst of a North wind.&#8221;</p><footer>Letter from Arles, 1888 (The Letters of a Post-Impressionist) &#183; tr. Anthony M. Ludovici, 1912 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/40393/pg40393-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>1 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I feel decidedly better in the South than in the North. I work even during the hour of noon, in the glaring sunlight, without a scrap of shade; and, believe me, I feel as happy as a cricket.&#8221;</p><footer>Letter from Arles, 1888 (The Letters of a Post-Impressionist) &#183; tr. Anthony M. Ludovici, 1912 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/40393/pg40393-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
